+# Analysis Report
+
+## Summary
+| Metric | Value |
+|--------|-------|
+| Approaches evaluated | 3 |
+| Top recommendation | Postgres-backed queue (SKIP LOCKED) |
+| Confidence | High |
+| Divergent assessments | 0 |
+
+## Context Weights
+| Dimension | Weight | Rationale |
+|-----------|--------|-----------|
+| Technical Soundness | 25% | Production-critical — writes real prices; material business risk if queue drops or duplicates |
+| Future Flexibility | 20% | Per-item loop pattern is reused by STORY-014 (inventory); must generalize cleanly |
+| Business Value | 20% | Clear pricing parity gap; cross-channel reporting is a core GC need |
+| Implementation Effort | 20% | 30-day staging cert window; no room for new-infra yak-shaving |
+| Risk Profile | 15% | Price writes carry financial risk but mitigated by staging-first + idempotency |
+
+## Evaluation Agents
+| Agent | Dimensions | Approaches Scored |
+|-------|-----------|------------------|
+| Technical | Soundness + Flexibility | 3 |
+| Business | Value + Effort | 3 |
+| Risk | Risk Profile + Register | 3 |
+
+## Approaches Evaluated
+
+### A. Postgres-backed queue (SKIP LOCKED)
+Use Postgres `FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED` as a lightweight job queue. The `target_price_queue` table holds inflight items; a worker process polls with `SKIP LOCKED`, sends per-item PUTs at rate-limit pace, and marks rows complete in the same transaction. No new infrastructure — same Postgres instance as the rest of the app.
+
+### B. Redis-backed queue (Celery + Redis)
+Add Redis as a message broker with Celery workers consuming pricing tasks. Each task corresponds to one per-item PUT. Celery handles retry, rate limiting via `celery.utils.time.rate` or a token-bucket plugin. Requires new Redis infrastructure + Celery process management.
+
+### C. ADF (Azure Data Factory) orchestrated
+Use Azure Data Factory pipelines to orchestrate the per-item pricing loop. ADF ForEach activity iterates over items, calls the Target API via HTTP connector, with retry/rate-limit handled by ADF's built-in policies. State lives in ADF run history.
+
+## Scoring Matrix
+| Approach | Technical (25%) | Flexibility (20%) | Value (20%) | Effort (20%) | Risk (15%) | Weighted |
+|----------|:-:|:-:|:-:|:-:|:-:|:-:|
+| A. Postgres queue | 5 | 4 | 5 | 5 | 4 | **4.65** |
+| B. Redis + Celery | 4 | 4 | 3 | 2 | 3 | **3.25** |
+| C. ADF orchestrated | 3 | 3 | 2 | 2 | 2 | **2.45** |
+
+## Divergent Assessments
+_No divergent assessments. All three evaluation dimensions converge on Approach A._
+
+## Top 3 Ranking
+
+### 1. Postgres-backed queue — SKIP LOCKED (Weighted: 4.65)
+- **Why #1:** Zero new infrastructure, transactional consistency with price history writes, and the simplest path to idempotency (unique constraint on queue table). Matches the project's tech stack decision (Python + FastAPI + Postgres).
+- **Key strength:** Atomic enqueue-dequeue-record cycle — the price PUT, queue dequeue, and `target_price_history` insert happen in one transaction. No distributed coordination needed.
+- **Key risk:** Postgres polling adds marginal DB load. Mitigated: Target's per-item rate limit (~1 req/sec per OQ-3 conservative default) means queue throughput is API-bound, not DB-bound. Even 10,000 queued items drain in ~3 hours at 1/s.
+- **Trade-off:** No real-time push semantics (polling interval introduces latency). Acceptable because Target pricing is near-real-time, not sub-second.
+
+### 2. Redis + Celery (Weighted: 3.25)
+- **Why #2:** Proven distributed task queue with built-in retry. Would be the right choice if throughput requirements were 100× higher.
+- **Key strength:** Push-based — worker picks up tasks immediately. Sub-second dispatch latency.
+- **Key risk:** New infrastructure (Redis) adds operational surface. Redis without AOF persistence can lose inflight tasks on restart. Celery configuration is non-trivial for rate-limited single-concurrency workers.
+- **Trade-off:** Gains real-time dispatch but adds an entire infrastructure dependency for a workload that's API-rate-limited to ~1 req/sec. Overengineered for this volume.
+
+### 3. ADF orchestrated (Weighted: 2.45)
+- **Why #3:** Viable for batch-oriented pricing loads but wrong fit for a per-item loop that needs idempotency and tight error handling.
+- **Key strength:** Built-in monitoring, retry policies, and Azure portal visibility.
+- **Key risk:** Azure lock-in, cannot run locally/in Docker, hard to test in CI. Cross-cutting concern: no other story in this project uses ADF — would be a one-off pattern.
+- **Trade-off:** Gets orchestration "for free" but loses local testability, Docker consistency, and team familiarity.
+
+## Risk Register Highlights (Top 3 approaches)
+| ID | Approach | Risk | Severity | Mitigation |
+|----|----------|------|----------|-----------|
+| R-A-1 | Postgres queue | Polling latency (seconds, not ms) | Low | Acceptable for pricing; 5s poll interval is fine at 1 req/s rate limit |
+| R-A-2 | Postgres queue | Table bloat if queue not vacuumed | Low | `SKIP LOCKED` rows are DELETEd or status-flagged after PUT; autovacuum handles rest |
+| R-A-3 | Postgres queue | OQ-3 unresolved rate limit | Medium | Conservative 1 req/s default; reconfigure when STORY-007 captures actual limit |
+| R-A-4 | Postgres queue | MAP price has no confirmed source | Medium | Default to operator UI input; NetSuite integration deferred until source confirmed |
+| R-A-5 | Postgres queue | Currency assumption (USD only) | Low | USD hardcoded; STORY-007 to verify; if multi-currency needed, add `currency` column later |
+| R-B-1 | Redis + Celery | Redis data loss on restart | High | Would require AOF persistence + sentinel; adds ops burden |
+| R-B-2 | Redis + Celery | New infra not in Docker Compose | Medium | Must add Redis service, health checks, Celery worker process |
+| R-C-1 | ADF orchestrated | Cannot run in Docker/CI | High | All other stories use local Docker; ADF breaks the pattern |
+| R-C-2 | ADF orchestrated | Azure lock-in | Medium | No other story uses ADF; one-off dependency |
+
+## Ancillary Design Decisions (Phase 4 scope per seed.md)
+
+### Retry Policy
+**Decision:** Exponential backoff with jitter, max 5 retries, base 2s.
+- On 429 (rate limited): honor `Retry-After` header if present; otherwise backoff.
+- On 5xx: retry with backoff.
+- On 4xx (not 429): fail permanently, log to `target_price_history` with error status. Operator review required.
+- Dead-letter: after 5 retries, move to `status = 'dead_letter'` in queue table. Operator alert via existing monitoring.
+
+### Idempotency Strategy
+**Decision:** Composite unique constraint on `(product_id, list_price, offer_price, map_price)` in the inflight queue table.
+- If a duplicate tuple is enqueued while one is already inflight, the INSERT is a no-op (ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING).
+- The PUT itself is naturally idempotent (same prices → same result).
+- Price history records the PUT response regardless, creating an audit trail.
+
+### Currency Handling
+**Decision:** USD-only, hardcoded. Column reserved for future `currency` field.
+- Target Plus is US-only marketplace. USD is the only plausible currency.
+- STORY-007 staging discovery will confirm. If multi-currency surfaces, add `currency` column via migration in that story.
+
+### MAP Price Source
+**Decision:** Operator UI input (manual), stored in `target_price_queue.map_price`. NetSuite integration deferred.
+- No confirmed NetSuite field maps to MAP price (Amazon has no analog).
+- Safest path: operator sets MAP in GC's UI. If NetSuite integration is later confirmed, add a sync job.
+- Phase 6 spec should include a nullable `map_price_source` enum (`manual`, `netsuite`) for future flexibility.
+
+## Recommendation
+
+**Postgres-backed queue with SKIP LOCKED.** This is the clear winner across all dimensions. It adds zero infrastructure, leverages the existing Postgres instance, provides transactional consistency for the enqueue→PUT→record cycle, and establishes the per-item loop pattern that STORY-014 (inventory) will inherit. The API rate limit (~1 req/s) means Postgres polling latency is irrelevant — the bottleneck is always the Target API, not the queue.
+
+Redis + Celery would be the right tool at 100× throughput. ADF is architecturally misaligned with the project's Docker-first, local-testable design.
+
+Retry: exponential backoff, max 5, with dead-letter. Idempotency: composite unique constraint + ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING. Currency: USD-only. MAP source: operator UI (manual) with future NetSuite hook.
